Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (e) In evaluating an Acquisition Proposal, the Company Board shall consider, in good faith, the following factors: (i) the financial terms of the proposal, (ii) the strategic benefits to the Company, (iii) the impact on the Company’s employees and other stakeholders, and (iv) any potential regulatory hurdles. The Company Board, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors of nationally recognized standing, shall determine whether the proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Company shall provide written notice to Parent of its intention to accept a Superior Proposal not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the third Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, during which period the Company shall negotiate in good faith with Parent to make adjustments to this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer be considered superior. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:

