Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (b) The Company Board, in evaluating any Acquisition Proposal, shall consider in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors of nationally recognized reputation, whether such proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. In making such determination, the Company Board shall take into account not only the financial terms of the proposal but also the strategic fit of the acquisition with the Company's long-term goals, potential regulatory hurdles, and the likelihood of obtaining necessary approvals. Additionally, the Company Board may consider the impact of the proposal on employees, customers, and other stakeholders. (c) The Company shall provide Parent with written notice of any determination by the Company Board that an Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, including details of the financial and non-financial terms that led to such determination. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:

