Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, prior to the Company Stockholder Meeting, the Company Board may change its recommendation if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors, that a failure to change its recommendation would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided the Parent with written notice of its intention to change the recommendation at least five Business Days in advance, and (iii) if not related to a change in applicable Law, the decision to change the recommendation is in connection with an Acquisition Proposal that the Company Board has determined, after consultation with its financial advisors and outside legal counsel, to be a Superior Proposal. Additionally, the Company Board must consider the impact of any regulatory requirements or potential shareholder litigation that could arise from accepting the Superior Proposal, ensuring that the decision aligns with both financial and legal considerations. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

