Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Board Evaluation. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, before the Company Board can make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change, the following must occur: (i) the Company Board must determine in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and its financial advisor, that the proposed Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, and that not acting on it would be inconsistent with their fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company Board must provide written notice to Parent of its intention to make such a change at least five Business Days prior, detailing the material terms of the Acquisition Proposal, and (iii) during the notice period, the Company Board must negotiate with Parent in good faith, considering any revisions or proposals made by Parent to amend the terms of this Agreement to make it more favorable. The financial terms of the Acquisition Proposal are a primary consideration, but the Company Board must also consider the legal implications and fiduciary responsibilities. Any amendments to the financial terms or any material changes to the Superior Proposal will require a new notice period of three Business Days. (Pages 102-103)\nAnswer: 
