Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: ARTICLE 8. Consideration of Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in this Agreement, the Company Board may effect a Company Recommendation Change only if: (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal; (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of its intention to effect a Company Recommendation Change at least four Business Days before doing so, which notice shall include the identity of the party making the Superior Proposal and the material terms thereof; (iii) the Company has negotiated in good faith with Parent during the notice period to allow Parent to propose amendments to the Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal ceases to be a Superior Proposal; (iv) after considering any amendments proposed by Parent, the Company Board determines, in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that the failure to make a Company Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law; and (v) the Company has obtained all necessary regulatory approvals and determined that the proposal complies with all applicable legal requirements. Any change to the financial terms or other material terms of a Superior Proposal shall require the Company to provide a new notice and comply with the requirements of this Section 8(c). (Pages 90-91)\nAnswer:
Choices:

