Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.5. No Solicitation. <omitted> (e) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, at any time prior to the Closing Date, the Company Board may make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board's intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change at least four Business Days prior to such change, (iii) the decision is in connection with an Acquisition Proposal, which the Company Board has determined in good faith, after consultation with its financial advisor and strategic advisors, constitutes a Superior Proposal, considering strategic fit, regulatory implications, and long-term growth potential, and (iv) the Company shall have complied with the negotiation obligations set forth in Section 8.5(f), including considering any proposed revisions by Parent. The Company Board shall have determined, after considering any such revisions, that the Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:

